(a) It shall be unlawful for any minor, 16 years of age or younger, to loiter, wander, stroll or play upon the public streets, highways, roads, alleys, parks, playgrounds or other public grounds, public places, public buildings, places of amusement, eating places, vacant lots or any place unsupervised by an adult having the lawful authority to be at such places, between the hours of 11:00 p.m. of any day and 6:00 a.m. of the following day; provided, however, that on Fridays and Saturdays the effective hours are between 12:00 midnight and 6:00 a.m. of the following day and, provided that the provisions of this subsection shall not apply in the following instances:
(1) When a minor is accompanied by his parent, guardian or other adult person having the lawful care and custody of the minor;
(2) When the minor is upon an emergency errand directed by his parent, guardian or other adult person having the lawful care and custody of such minor;
(3) When the minor is returning directly home from a school activity, entertainment, recreational activity or dance;
(4) When the minor is returning directly home from lawful employment that makes it necessary to be in such places during the prescribed period of time;
(5) When the minor is attending or traveling directly to or from an activity involving the exercise of free speech, freedom of assembly or free exercise of religion.
(b) It shall be unlawful for the parent, guardian or other person having custody or control of any child 16 years of age or younger to permit or, by insufficient control, to allow such child to be in or upon the public streets or any other places listed in subsection (a) of this section within the city between the hours of 11:00 p.m. on any day and 6:00 a.m. the following day, or on Fridays and Saturdays between the hours of 12:00 midnight and 6:00 a.m. the following day, except in circumstances set out in subsections (a)(1) through (5) of this section. Upon conviction of a violation of this subsection for the first time, a person shall be given a warning citation. Upon further convictions, a person shall be subject to punishment for a violation of this section.
(Code 1982, § 15-3.1)

599:
The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this chapter, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Picketing means the act of standing upon or patrolling any public street, sidewalk, public property or other public place where persons may lawfully assemble, by one or more persons, whether or not carrying signs or placards, whether the picketing is done in an organized or disorganized manner or is authorized or unauthorized by a responsible party, for the purpose of:
(1) Persuading other persons to cease patronizing, contracting with or employing any person or business establishment;
(2) Persuading other persons to cease certain employment practices;
(3) Calling attention, for any reason whatsoever, to any business practice or other practice followed by the business being picketed;
(4) Giving publicity to, or communicating information regarding the existence of facts or merits of, a labor dispute or other controversy of any kind, whether or not labor oriented.
(Code 1982, § 15-5)
Cross reference— Definitions generally, § 1-2.

600:
Any person engaged in picketing on any public right-of-way or other public property within the city shall adhere to the following requirements:
(1) Picketing participants must keep moving at all times;
(2) Space between individual participants in picketing shall be at least ten feet and such individuals shall remain in single file at all times;
(3) If placards or banners are carried, they must be held so as not to endanger any pedestrian or other person and so as not to interfere with the freedom of movement of any person;
(4) No placard or banner shall be used or carried in a threatening or menacing manner;
(5) Participants in picketing shall not unreasonably interfere with, or obstruct in any way, pedestrian or vehicular traffic;
(6) Participants in picketing shall not block, obstruct or unreasonably interfere in any way with ingress to and egress from any building or structure;
(7) The number of participants in picketing shall be limited to such numbers as will not unreasonably obstruct or interfere with pedestrian or vehicular traffic;
(8) No pedestrian shall be forced to go into any portion of a street in order to avoid coming in physical contact with participants in picketing;
(9) Bystanders shall be restricted to two monitors per business establishment picketed.
(Code 1982, § 15-6)
State Law reference— Unlawful assembly, O.C.G.A. § 16-11-33; obstructing highways, O.C.G.A. § 16-11-43; picketing, O.C.G.A. § 34-6-2 et seq.

601:
No permit from the city is required for picketing on public rights-of-way or property; however, persons desiring to picket are required to notify the police department of:
(1) Their intention to picket;
(2) The locations of the picketing;
(3) The dates and times of the picketing; and
(4) The names, addresses and telephone numbers of at least two local residents designated as agents by the organization sponsoring or encouraging such picketing.
(Code 1982, § 15-7)

609:
(a) Any person desiring a registration card as required by section 66-2 shall file an application with the chief of police. Such application shall give:
(1) The applicant's full name and home address;
(2) The name of the last three municipalities in which the applicant worked, if any;
(3) The name of the company or individual such applicant represents or works for, if any;
(4) The merchandise, services or products to be sold, disposed of, peddled or hawked by the applicant;
